hyperactivity-is-the-theory-of-knowledge-graphs-lines-62 = cons "A decentralized knowledge graph is not a client/server protocol, a REST schema, or a central database with caches in front of it. In Part I, interaction is the sole primitive (§4.5): a local computation is a process over a command/response signature `Sig` composed with a handler `o-handle` (§7.6), reading immutable objects by content key. Networked operation is the same interaction with a wire crossing a network port: two peers form a composed process `ParState` with directed channels (`par-D-le-N`), exchanging definitions, proofs, and dependency closures across that boundary. In `lib/pool.cub`, this is proved directly as a theorem over `StoreSig`: `eval-peer` composes a local handler `h-peer` with a remote peer handler `h-local`, checking every remote response at the boundary (`ca-check`), and `peer-transparent` proves that for every computation `p : Step (StoreSig K V) A` and valid peer store `rem`, `eval-peer loc rem chk p ≡ eval-local (ca-union loc rem) p` — network execution is path-equal to local execution over the combined store." (cons "" nil)
